    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Most modern historians generally view the United Nations as a mixed success in the 21st century, with its ability to prevent major great power wars limited by the geopolitical obstacles of the P5 veto power. While the UN has been highly effective in containing regional conflicts and promoting peacekeeping in specific \"hotspots\" like the Balkans and parts of Africa, its failure to prevent atrocities or intervene decisively in regions like Syria and Myanmar has highlighted significant structural weaknesses. Consequently, scholars often argue that the organization currently prioritizes the maintenance of international order over robust intervention, relying more on consensus than enforcement to avoid escalating tensions among permanent member states.",
      "source": "target",
